# Complete EKS Guide — Cluster, Microservice, and What to Learn Next

This is one complete document. It covers:

1. **Part 1** — Create an EKS cluster and deploy a microservice (step by step)
2. **Part 2** — What breaks in production with that basic setup, and how to fix it (Helm, IRSA, Ingress, Karpenter)

Each step explains *what* you are doing and *why* — so you understand it, not just copy it.

---

# PART 1 — Create an EKS Cluster and Deploy a Microservice

---

## What We Are Building

```
Your Laptop
    │
    │ push image
    ▼
ECR (AWS image storage)
    │
    │ pull image
    ▼
EKS Cluster (demo-test)
    ├── Node 1 (EC2)
    │     └── Pod → your microservice
    └── Node 2 (EC2)
          └── Pod → your microservice
                │
                ▼
         LoadBalancer → Internet
```

> **Quick mental model:**
> - **EKS** = AWS runs the Kubernetes "brain" for you
> - **Node** = EC2 server where your app runs
> - **Pod** = one running copy of your app
> - **Deployment** = "keep N copies of my app alive always"
> - **Service** = stable address so traffic reaches your pods

---

## Step 0 — Prerequisites

- AWS account with permission to create EKS, EC2, IAM, VPC, ECR, CloudFormation
- A terminal (Linux, macOS, or WSL on Windows)
- ~30–40 minutes (mostly waiting for AWS)

---

## Step 1 — Install the Tools

### macOS
```bash
brew install awscli eksctl kubernetes-cli
# Docker Desktop: https://www.docker.com/products/docker-desktop
```

### Linux (Ubuntu / Debian)
```bash
# AWS CLI
curl "https://awscli.amazonaws.com/awscli-exe-linux-x86_64.zip" -o awscliv2.zip
unzip awscliv2.zip && sudo ./aws/install

# eksctl
curl --silent --location "https://github.com/eksctl-io/eksctl/releases/latest/download/eksctl_$(uname -s)_amd64.tar.gz" | tar xz -C /tmp
sudo mv /tmp/eksctl /usr/local/bin

# kubectl
curl -LO "https://dl.k8s.io/release/$(curl -L -s https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt)/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl"
sudo install -o root -g root -m 0755 kubectl /usr/local/bin/kubectl

# Docker
sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y docker.io

# Allow docker without sudo (required — otherwise docker commands fail)
sudo usermod -aG docker $USER
newgrp docker
```

### Verify
```bash
aws --version && eksctl version && kubectl version --client && docker --version
```

