## Step 3 — Create the EKS Cluster

One command builds everything: VPC, IAM roles, control plane, and worker nodes.

```bash
eksctl create cluster \
  --name demo-test \
  --region ap-south-1 \
  --version 1.34 \
  --node-type t3.medium \
  --nodes 2 \
  --nodes-min 2 \
  --nodes-max 4 \
  --managed
```

**What each flag means:**

| Flag | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `--name demo-test` | Cluster name |
| `--region ap-south-1` | Mumbai region |
| `--version 1.34` | Kubernetes version |
| `--node-type t3.medium` | EC2 size for worker nodes |
| `--nodes 2` | Start with 2 nodes |
| `--nodes-min/max 2/4` | ASG bounds — does NOT auto-scale without Cluster Autoscaler or Karpenter |
| `--managed` | AWS manages node patching and updates |

> ⏳ Takes 15–20 minutes. eksctl auto-updates your kubeconfig when done.

```bash
# Verify cluster is alive
kubectl get nodes
# Should show 2 nodes with status Ready
```

