## Step 4 — Build the Microservice

```bash
mkdir my-microservice && cd my-microservice
```

### `app.js`
```javascript
const http = require('http');

const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' });
  res.end(JSON.stringify({
    message: 'Hello from my microservice on EKS!',
    hostname: require('os').hostname()   // shows which pod answered
  }));
});

server.listen(3000, () => console.log('Service running on port 3000'));
```

### `Dockerfile`
```dockerfile
FROM node:20-alpine
WORKDIR /app
COPY app.js .
EXPOSE 3000
CMD ["node", "app.js"]
```

No `npm install` needed — we only use Node's built-in modules.

---

## Step 5 — Push Image to ECR

ECR (Elastic Container Registry) is AWS's private Docker registry. The cluster pulls images from here.

```bash
# 5a. Create the repository
aws ecr create-repository --repository-name my-microservice --region ap-south-1

# 5b. Set registry variables
ACCOUNT_ID=$(aws sts get-caller-identity --query Account --output text)
REGION=ap-south-1
REGISTRY=$ACCOUNT_ID.dkr.ecr.$REGION.amazonaws.com
echo $REGISTRY   # save this — you need it in Step 6

# 5c. Log Docker in to ECR
aws ecr get-login-password --region $REGION | docker login --username AWS --password-stdin $REGISTRY

# 5d. Build, tag, push
docker build -t my-microservice .
docker tag my-microservice:latest $REGISTRY/my-microservice:latest
docker push $REGISTRY/my-microservice:latest
```

> 💡 Mac M-series chip? Build with: `docker build --platform linux/amd64 -t my-microservice .`

---

## Step 6 — Write Kubernetes YAML

### `deployment.yaml`

First create the file:

```yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: my-microservice
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: my-microservice
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: my-microservice
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: my-microservice
        image: REGISTRY_PLACEHOLDER/my-microservice:latest
        ports:
        - containerPort: 3000
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: "100m"
            memory: "128Mi"
          limits:
            cpu: "250m"
            memory: "256Mi"
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: my-microservice
spec:
  type: LoadBalancer
  selector:
    app: my-microservice
  ports:
  - port: 80
    targetPort: 3000
```

Then inject the real registry address:

```bash
# Safety check — if this prints nothing, re-run Step 5b first
echo "Registry: $REGISTRY"

# Replace placeholder (works on both Linux and macOS)
sed -i.bak "s|REGISTRY_PLACEHOLDER|$REGISTRY|g" deployment.yaml

# Confirm it looks right
grep "image:" deployment.yaml
# Should show: image: 123456789.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/my-microservice:latest
```

---

## Step 7 — Deploy

```bash
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml

# Watch pods start (Ctrl+C to stop)
kubectl get pods -w
# Wait until both show Running
```

