# PART 2 — What Breaks in Production, and How to Fix It

---

## The Problem with Our Basic Setup

The setup from Part 1 works. But it has 4 real problems in production:

```
Problem 1: 1 LoadBalancer per service = ₹1,500/month per service
Problem 2: Nodes run 24/7 at fixed cost even at 3 AM with zero traffic
Problem 3: 5 services = 5 copy-pasted YAML files, impossible to maintain
Problem 4: Pods need AWS access → developers put keys directly in YAML = security risk
```

These 4 problems have 4 solutions — in this exact learning order:

```
1. Helm          → fix Problem 3 first (you'll use Helm to install 2, 3, 4)
2. IRSA          → fix Problem 4 (security before anything touches AWS)
3. Ingress + ALB → fix Problem 1 (one load balancer for everything)
4. Karpenter     → fix Problem 2 (pay only for nodes you actually need)
```

---

## 1st — Helm (Fix: Stop Copy-Pasting YAML)

### Why You Need It

Right now if you have 5 microservices, you have 5 `deployment.yaml` files that all look 95% the same. Change one thing — add a liveness probe — and you must edit 5 files. You miss one. Production breaks.

Also, everything else in this guide (IRSA setup, Ingress controller, Karpenter) is installed via Helm. Learn it first.

```
Without Helm:                         With Helm:
  api-deployment.yaml (copy-paste)      One chart template
  auth-deployment.yaml (copy-paste)     api/values.yaml   → name: api, port: 3001
  pay-deployment.yaml  (copy-paste)     auth/values.yaml  → name: auth, port: 3002
  change 1 thing = edit 3 files        pay/values.yaml   → name: pay, port: 3003
  miss 1 = production breaks           change 1 thing = edit template once
```

### Install Helm

```bash
# macOS
brew install helm

# Linux
curl https://raw.githubusercontent.com/helm/helm/main/scripts/get-helm-3 | bash

# Verify
helm version
```

### Create a Chart for Your Microservice

```bash
helm create my-microservice
```

This generates:
```
my-microservice/
├── Chart.yaml        ← chart name and version
├── values.yaml       ← your config variables
└── templates/
    ├── deployment.yaml   ← uses {{ .Values.xxx }} variables
    ├── service.yaml
    └── ingress.yaml
```

Edit `values.yaml` — this is the only file that changes per service:

```yaml
replicaCount: 2

image:
  repository: 123456789.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/my-microservice
  tag: latest
  pullPolicy: Always

service:
  type: ClusterIP
  port: 80
  targetPort: 3000

resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "100m"
    memory: "128Mi"
  limits:
    cpu: "250m"
    memory: "256Mi"
```

### Deploy and Manage with Helm

```bash
helm install my-microservice ./my-microservice          # first deploy
helm upgrade my-microservice ./my-microservice \
  --set image.tag=v2.0.0                                # update to new version
helm upgrade --install my-microservice ./my-microservice # install OR update (safe default)
helm rollback my-microservice 1                         # rollback to previous version
helm list                                               # see all deployed charts
helm uninstall my-microservice                          # remove everything
```

### Multiple Environments (Dev / Staging / Prod)

```bash
# One chart, separate values per environment
helm upgrade --install my-microservice ./my-microservice \
  -f values.yaml \
  -f values-staging.yaml \
  --namespace staging

helm upgrade --install my-microservice ./my-microservice \
  -f values.yaml \
  -f values-prod.yaml \
  --namespace prod
```

Same chart. Different values. No copy-paste.

