## 2nd — IRSA (Fix: No More Hardcoded AWS Keys)

### Why You Need It

Your microservice needs to talk to AWS — read from S3, write to DynamoDB, send SQS messages.

The wrong way (very common, very dangerous):

```yaml
# ❌ NEVER DO THIS
env:
- name: AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID
  value: "AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE"
- name: AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
  value: "wJalrXUtnFEMI/K7MDENG/bPxRfiCYEXAMPLEKEY"
```

If anyone gets your YAML or your Git repo, they have full AWS access. Keys never expire.

**IRSA (IAM Roles for Service Accounts)** is the right way. Each pod gets a temporary AWS identity that auto-rotates every hour. No keys in YAML, ever.

```
Without IRSA:                    With IRSA:
  Hardcoded keys in YAML    →     No keys anywhere in YAML
  Keys never expire         →     Credentials auto-rotate every hour
  One leaked YAML = full    →     Pod can only access what its
  AWS access                       IAM policy explicitly allows
```

### Set Up IRSA

**Step 1: Enable OIDC (one-time per cluster)**
```bash
# This links your cluster to AWS IAM
eksctl utils associate-iam-oidc-provider \
  --cluster demo-test \
  --region ap-south-1 \
  --approve
```

**Step 2: Create a policy for what your pod is allowed to do**
```bash
cat > pod-s3-policy.json << EOF
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject", "s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::cashify-app-data",
        "arn:aws:s3:::cashify-app-data/*"
      ]
    }
  ]
}
EOF

aws iam create-policy \
  --policy-name my-microservice-s3-policy \
  --policy-document file://pod-s3-policy.json
```

**Step 3: Create a service account linked to that policy**
```bash
ACCOUNT_ID=$(aws sts get-caller-identity --query Account --output text)

eksctl create iamserviceaccount \
  --cluster demo-test \
  --region ap-south-1 \
  --namespace default \
  --name my-microservice-sa \
  --attach-policy-arn arn:aws:iam::${ACCOUNT_ID}:policy/my-microservice-s3-policy \
  --approve
```

**Step 4: Tell your pod to use this service account**
```yaml
spec:
  template:
    spec:
      serviceAccountName: my-microservice-sa   # ← add this one line
      containers:
      - name: my-microservice
        image: ...
        # No AWS keys. Credentials arrive automatically.
```

**Verify inside the pod:**
```bash
kubectl exec -it $(kubectl get pod -l app=my-microservice -o name | head -1) -- sh
# Inside the pod:
aws s3 ls s3://cashify-app-data    # works without any keys configured
```

